WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.301 --> 00:00:09.440
The Earth is producing about a third
more power from the sun

00:00:09.473 --> 00:00:11.208
this spring than last.

00:00:11.675 --> 00:00:15.446
That solar power is suddenly
so cheap and abundant

00:00:15.479 --> 00:00:19.750
that Australians will soon get
three hours of electricity

00:00:19.784 --> 00:00:22.386
free every afternoon, free.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:26.190
After 400,000 years
of working hard for energy,

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:29.827
from gathering firewood
to paying the power bill,

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:34.999
some humans will suddenly have access
to this commodity for nothing.

00:00:35.499 --> 00:00:39.403
California, fourth largest
economy in the world,

00:00:39.437 --> 00:00:44.408
now produces most days
more than 100 percent of its electricity

00:00:44.442 --> 00:00:45.876
from renewable energy.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:48.546
At night, when the sun goes down,

00:00:48.579 --> 00:00:53.918
big new batteries on the grid
turn dark into daylight.

00:00:53.951 --> 00:00:56.554
In Pakistan, peasant farmers

00:00:56.587 --> 00:01:00.658
who used to pay through the nose
for diesel power

00:01:00.691 --> 00:01:02.960
to run their irrigation pumps

00:01:02.993 --> 00:01:05.629
are using 30 percent less of the fuel

00:01:05.629 --> 00:01:08.399
than they were two years ago,

00:01:08.432 --> 00:01:13.237
in large part because they've imported
millions of cheap Chinese solar panels

00:01:13.237 --> 00:01:17.608
and gone on YouTube
to figure out how to connect them up.

00:01:17.641 --> 00:01:22.480
They often lack the money
to build the steel scaffolding

00:01:22.513 --> 00:01:24.782
we're used to seeing at solar farms.

00:01:24.815 --> 00:01:25.983
No matter.

00:01:26.016 --> 00:01:29.720
They just lay them on the ground
and point them at the sun.

00:01:30.087 --> 00:01:36.927
In a world where many, many big things
have taken a very dark turn this decade,

00:01:36.961 --> 00:01:40.898
this is the one big thing
that is going right.

00:01:40.931 --> 00:01:44.602
We have quite suddenly
the opportunity to build a clean,

00:01:44.635 --> 00:01:48.005
abundant future if we simply seize it.

00:01:48.038 --> 00:01:52.276
But most of us don't recognize
how huge this moment is.

00:01:52.743 --> 00:01:55.579
In part, that's because
we've spent decades

00:01:55.579 --> 00:01:58.482
calling this stuff “alternative energy,”

00:01:58.516 --> 00:02:03.420
and it's blinded us to the fact
that it's now the obvious, common-sense,

00:02:03.454 --> 00:02:06.524
straightforward way to power the future.

00:02:07.358 --> 00:02:12.163
Roughly 90 percent of new electric
generation around the world last year

00:02:12.196 --> 00:02:13.931
came from the sun and the wind.

00:02:13.931 --> 00:02:17.668
And that, in turn,
is because earlier this decade,

00:02:17.701 --> 00:02:21.205
we crossed some invisible line
where it became cheaper

00:02:21.205 --> 00:02:23.440
to produce power from the sun

00:02:23.474 --> 00:02:26.110
than from burning coal and gas and oil.

00:02:26.143 --> 00:02:27.611
Let me repeat,

00:02:27.645 --> 00:02:31.715
we live on a planet
where the cheapest way to make power

00:02:31.715 --> 00:02:35.219
is to point a sheet of glass at the sun.

00:02:35.252 --> 00:02:39.456
(Applause)

00:02:39.456 --> 00:02:44.395
If we can keep the staggering growth
of the last 36 months going,

00:02:44.428 --> 00:02:47.064
then we could enter
an extraordinary new age.

00:02:47.097 --> 00:02:48.532
Consider.

00:02:48.566 --> 00:02:51.068
Eight or nine million people a year die

00:02:51.101 --> 00:02:54.271
from breathing the combustion
byproducts of fossil fuels.

00:02:54.271 --> 00:02:57.074
That's one death in five on this planet.

00:02:57.541 --> 00:02:59.210
But the Chinese cities,

00:02:59.243 --> 00:03:04.014
whose air was so foul a few years ago
that you couldn't see across the street,

00:03:04.048 --> 00:03:05.883
are suddenly much cleaner.

00:03:05.916 --> 00:03:09.186
Most of the cars sold there now
come with a plug,

00:03:09.186 --> 00:03:12.756
and it connects them to the vast
solar and wind farms

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:16.393
that the Chinese are building
faster than anyone on Earth.

00:03:16.427 --> 00:03:17.962
More fundamentally,

00:03:17.995 --> 00:03:21.632
this energy transition
is our only real chance

00:03:21.632 --> 00:03:25.703
of even starting to catch up
with the physics of climate change.

00:03:25.736 --> 00:03:29.039
It's too late to stop global warming.

00:03:29.073 --> 00:03:30.641
But it's not too late

00:03:30.674 --> 00:03:35.212
to shave tenths of a degree off
how hot the planet [will] eventually get.

00:03:35.246 --> 00:03:37.348
And each 10th of a degree that we raise

00:03:37.381 --> 00:03:41.352
the temperature moves
100 million of our brothers and sisters

00:03:41.385 --> 00:03:45.623
out of a safe climate zone
and into a more perilous one.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:49.760
This new age would come
with one other great bonus.

00:03:49.793 --> 00:03:53.731
Unlike fossil fuels,
which are concentrated in a few places,

00:03:53.764 --> 00:03:56.500
the sun's energy is available everywhere.

00:03:56.867 --> 00:04:01.305
Instead of, say, attacking
Venezuela to secure its oil

00:04:01.338 --> 00:04:04.308
or threatening to cut
off Europe's gas supply

00:04:04.341 --> 00:04:06.910
to force the subjugation of Ukraine,

00:04:06.944 --> 00:04:08.612
we get to imagine a world

00:04:08.646 --> 00:04:12.416
where hydrocarbons
no longer equal wealth and power.

00:04:12.449 --> 00:04:15.686
Imagine how many fewer wars and coups

00:04:15.719 --> 00:04:19.123
and terrorist plots we would have seen
in the last century

00:04:19.156 --> 00:04:21.759
if oil had been of trivial value.

00:04:22.293 --> 00:04:27.498
As this spring has reminded us,
humans are all too good at starting wars.

00:04:27.498 --> 00:04:28.766
But even for us,

00:04:28.799 --> 00:04:33.103
figuring out how to fight one
over sunshine will be tricky.

00:04:33.137 --> 00:04:38.509
Sunlight travels 93 million miles
to reach the Earth,

00:04:38.542 --> 00:04:41.879
but none of those miles go
through the Strait of Hormuz.

00:04:42.212 --> 00:04:48.652
(Applause)

00:04:48.686 --> 00:04:50.387
So what’s good news?

00:04:50.921 --> 00:04:54.758
Unless, of course, you own
an oil well or a coal mine.

00:04:55.292 --> 00:04:59.096
Then the rise of clean energy
becomes an extraordinary threat,

00:04:59.129 --> 00:05:01.665
one that mobilizes you to fight back.

00:05:01.699 --> 00:05:03.400
In the US, for instance,

00:05:03.434 --> 00:05:07.604
candidate Trump told the oil industry
that if they donated huge sums,

00:05:07.638 --> 00:05:09.206
he would have their back.

00:05:09.239 --> 00:05:10.774
In his first year in office,

00:05:10.808 --> 00:05:14.445
he shut down huge wind farms
off the eastern seaboard [and]

00:05:14.445 --> 00:05:16.880
killed solar farms across the country.

00:05:17.348 --> 00:05:22.653
Now Trump can’t stop
clean energy single-handedly.

00:05:22.686 --> 00:05:25.155
It's not like he's Jesus or something.

00:05:25.189 --> 00:05:27.358
(Laughter)

00:05:27.391 --> 00:05:30.728
(Applause and cheers)

00:05:30.728 --> 00:05:34.598
Indeed, the economics
are so favorable that even in the US,

00:05:34.631 --> 00:05:38.602
most new electric generation last year
came from the sun and the wind.

00:05:38.635 --> 00:05:42.606
And the biggest growth
was in the state of Texas,

00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:44.808
the home ground of big oil.

00:05:45.242 --> 00:05:47.945
But Trump and Russia and Saudi Arabia

00:05:47.945 --> 00:05:52.616
and a handful of other world powers
may be able to slow that shift.

00:05:52.983 --> 00:05:56.887
America's Secretary of Energy,
against all global evidence,

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:59.356
has said there's no climate crisis,

00:05:59.356 --> 00:06:02.793
and we're not in the middle
of an energy transition, either.

00:06:02.793 --> 00:06:05.529
So he's been subsidizing coal

00:06:05.562 --> 00:06:09.233
instead of embracing
the clean energy that would, say,

00:06:09.266 --> 00:06:14.104
let us build any data centers
we actually needed quickly and cheaply.

00:06:14.138 --> 00:06:16.673
As the Chinese have amply demonstrated,

00:06:16.707 --> 00:06:20.144
solar fields can sprout
in a matter of weeks.

00:06:21.011 --> 00:06:26.283
America’s effort to delay this transition
is not just economically stupid --

00:06:26.316 --> 00:06:30.387
it also endangers the last,
best hope of cooling this Earth.

00:06:30.421 --> 00:06:31.955
Sure, in 40 years,

00:06:31.989 --> 00:06:34.958
we will run the planet
on sun and wind because it's cheap.

00:06:34.992 --> 00:06:38.228
But if it takes us anything
like 40 years to get there,

00:06:38.228 --> 00:06:41.899
then the planet we run on sun and wind
will be a broken planet.

00:06:42.499 --> 00:06:45.135
So those of us who hope
for a different future

00:06:45.169 --> 00:06:46.670
need to fight for it.

00:06:46.703 --> 00:06:48.338
And with the cheap solar panel,

00:06:48.372 --> 00:06:51.642
we finally have a real weapon
in that struggle.

00:06:51.675 --> 00:06:53.410
We need to push in every country

00:06:53.444 --> 00:06:55.145
and at every level of government

00:06:55.179 --> 00:06:58.682
for a faster move
toward cheap, clean power.

00:06:59.116 --> 00:07:02.753
We started Sun Day in the US last year

00:07:02.753 --> 00:07:05.489
on the autumnal equinox across the nation,

00:07:05.489 --> 00:07:08.725
500 events -- from solar-powered concerts

00:07:08.759 --> 00:07:12.896
to the installation of solar panels
on Habitat for Humanity homes

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.566
helped spread this new energy gospel.

00:07:15.599 --> 00:07:20.237
We'll do it again
on the summer solstice in 2027,

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:23.073
and this time we will do it globally.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:28.078
Days like this help us build
the political power we need

00:07:28.111 --> 00:07:30.380
to push these things ahead.

00:07:30.914 --> 00:07:32.516
Americans, for instance,

00:07:32.549 --> 00:07:38.322
right now, pay three times what Europeans
or Australians pay for solar power,

00:07:38.355 --> 00:07:43.093
mostly because of unnecessary
bureaucracy at the state and local level.

00:07:43.126 --> 00:07:48.765
So Free the Rooftop is a cry that appeals
to conservatives as much as liberals.

00:07:48.799 --> 00:07:51.869
Utah became the first
American state last year

00:07:51.869 --> 00:07:55.205
to legalize small-scale plug-in solar,

00:07:55.239 --> 00:07:58.041
opening the door for the kind
of simple installations

00:07:58.075 --> 00:08:01.812
that have sprouted on the balconies
of millions of European apartments

00:08:01.812 --> 00:08:03.480
in the last three years.

00:08:03.514 --> 00:08:06.450
Maine and Virginia joined Utah last week.

00:08:07.217 --> 00:08:09.887
So yeah, as we push ahead with this work,

00:08:09.887 --> 00:08:12.923
we need to talk
about its scientific necessity

00:08:12.956 --> 00:08:15.025
and its economic pragmatism.

00:08:15.058 --> 00:08:18.128
But we have one other ace to play,

00:08:18.161 --> 00:08:21.865
and that's the sheer beauty
of power from the sun.

00:08:21.899 --> 00:08:25.402
We have a deep human connection
to our local star.

00:08:25.736 --> 00:08:30.374
We don't know for sure how
prehistoric people thought about things

00:08:30.374 --> 00:08:34.011
because, being prehistoric,
they failed to write it down.

00:08:34.044 --> 00:08:37.714
But we do know that every pile
of rocks they left behind,

00:08:37.748 --> 00:08:42.152
like Stonehenge, seems to point
to the equinox or the solstice.

00:08:43.053 --> 00:08:46.290
Do you not break into an involuntary grin

00:08:46.323 --> 00:08:50.861
when the sun comes out from behind a cloud
and hits you on the shoulder blades?

00:08:50.894 --> 00:08:52.763
If you don't get enough sun,

00:08:52.796 --> 00:08:58.235
you literally can get SAD --
seasonal affective disorder.

00:08:58.669 --> 00:09:02.139
So the mantra for this moment is clear:

00:09:02.172 --> 00:09:05.242
energy from heaven, not from hell.

00:09:05.909 --> 00:09:12.516
(Cheers and applause)

00:09:12.549 --> 00:09:16.620
And with that slogan,
what a breakthrough moment this could be.

00:09:16.653 --> 00:09:21.058
I mean, humans have been burning
things since the dawn of time.

00:09:21.091 --> 00:09:22.893
Darwin said language and fire

00:09:22.926 --> 00:09:25.829
were the two things
that set our species apart.

00:09:25.829 --> 00:09:27.397
And fire was mostly good.

00:09:27.431 --> 00:09:29.900
We learned to cook,
which gave us the big brain.

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:32.669
We could move north
and south from the equator.

00:09:32.703 --> 00:09:34.304
Anthropologists even think

00:09:34.338 --> 00:09:37.774
that eons of standing
around the campfire every night

00:09:37.808 --> 00:09:40.077
helped explain some of the social bonds

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:41.511
that mark our species.

00:09:41.545 --> 00:09:44.514
Think of it as a sort
of primitive TED talk.

00:09:44.548 --> 00:09:45.549
(Laughter)

00:09:45.549 --> 00:09:50.153
When we learned to control
the combustion of coal and oil and gas,

00:09:50.187 --> 00:09:53.824
that was the Industrial Revolution,
and it gave us modernity.

00:09:54.424 --> 00:09:59.763
But now the cost of those fires
has simply grown too high.

00:09:59.763 --> 00:10:03.166
The very future of the climate
on which we depend

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:06.003
is being overwhelmed by their smoke.

00:10:06.036 --> 00:10:09.606
So what sweet relief
that in this exact moment,

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:11.308
we can, if we want,

00:10:11.308 --> 00:10:13.076
put out those fires,

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:14.945
the big ones in our power plants,

00:10:14.978 --> 00:10:19.416
the smaller ones in our furnaces
and under the hoods of our cars.

00:10:19.449 --> 00:10:23.720
The sun already gives us light and warmth

00:10:23.754 --> 00:10:26.456
and, via photosynthesis, our supper.

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:31.061
Now it is willing to provide us
with all the power we could ever need.

00:10:31.061 --> 00:10:35.399
This gift has come
at the last possible moment.

00:10:35.432 --> 00:10:38.902
It would be an extraordinary
sin to waste it,

00:10:38.902 --> 00:10:42.706
and it would be a beautiful,
beautiful project

00:10:42.739 --> 00:10:45.142
for humans to build it out

00:10:45.175 --> 00:10:50.313
across our hot and tired Earth
in the next few years.

00:10:50.614 --> 00:10:54.751
In advance of your
participation in that fight,

00:10:54.751 --> 00:10:56.787
let me just say, thank you.

00:10:57.354 --> 00:11:03.727
(Cheers and applause)

